  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 132 PM MDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nThe strong upper level high will continue to slowly build \nnorthward today and Saturday.\u00a0Water vapor satellite imagery and \nACARS soundings show the airmass has dried as the ridge nears with\nprecipitable water values of 0.75-1.00 (in) across the Front \nRange and eastern plains. These values are near to slightly above \nnormal. Scattered showers and thunderstorms are expected over the \nhigher terrain this afternoon and early\u00a0evening. The last few runs\nof the HRRR model, including the 18Z run, have trended toward \nscattered thunderstorms for areas north of Denver, thus increased \nPoPs for this area.\u00a0\n\nThe upper level ridge will be centered over the Central Rockies \nthis weekend, leading to hot temperatures. Highs are expected to \nreach the upper 90s and lower 100s both days. A Heat Advisory is \nalready in effect. The record high for Denver on Saturday is 99 \ndegrees and on Sunday it is 100 degrees. Both of these records \ncould be tied or broken. Moisture remains near to slightly above \nnormal this weekend. Thus, we'll see scattered afternoon/evening \nshowers and thunderstorms, most numerous over the higher terrain. \nWarm mid level temperatures will act to cap the airmass over the \nfar eastern plains, but some of these storms should survive as \nthey cross the urban corridor. Heavy rain and wind gusts up to 50 \nmph will be the main threats with the thunderstorms.\u00a0\u00a0\n\nOn Monday, the upper level high shifts south of Colorado and \nremains south of Colorado through Wednesday. This will lead to an \nincrease in monsoonal moisture. Cloud cover will increase as well \nand temperatures are expected to cool somewhat, with highs in the \nupper 80s and 90s across northeast Colorado. Precipitable water \nvalues will be 140 to 200 percent of normal Monday through \nWednesday leading to scattered to numerous showers and \nthunderstorms. Heavy rainfall and gusty outflow winds will be the \nmain threats.\u00a0\n\nFor Thursday and Friday, the high is expected to be centered over\nthe Great Basin with a north to northwest flow aloft over \nColorado. Models still support scattered to numerous thunderstorms\nas monsoonal moisture continues over the region. Though there \ncould be a slight decrease in moisture and storms late next week. \nTemperatures are expected to be near to slightly above normal with\nhighs in the lower to mid 90s.",
